zune-jpeg: restore the component-scan rows a suspended attempt wrote

In the multi-scan baseline path (component scans buffered for the whole
image) an incremental attempt can decode rows past its last checkpoint from
the zero padding the bit reader appends at the end of the visible data: a
row whose start already has look-ahead past the end gets no checkpoint of
its own. The retry resumes from the earlier checkpoint, and when the now
visible data ends the scan at a marker before those rows, their padding
blocks stay in the buffered plane where a one-shot decode has the samples
from before the scan ("incremental pixels diverge from one-shot decode").
Save each scan row before an attempt writes it, starting a new backup at
every checkpoint (`save_multi_scan_row`), and put the rows back when
resuming from that checkpoint (`restore_multi_scan_rows`). Only incremental
or retried decodes of multi-scan images do the copy. Includes a regression
test (tests/incremental_resume.rs).

Not fixed upstream as of zune-image 2cdbbf5.
Upstream: open #506 (recover sequential component scans) mentions rolling
back speculative row writes on suspension in the same path; not merged.

